would fall in my room.

783
00:44:50,421 --> 00:44:52,787
Frank:
I can show you the way.

784
00:44:54,058 --> 00:44:56,185
(bells ringing)

785
00:45:05,469 --> 00:45:08,199
- Donnie: Dr. Monnitoff?
- Donnie.

786
00:45:08,272 --> 00:45:11,105
I know this is gonna sound
kind of weird,

787
00:45:11,175 --> 00:45:14,235
but... do you know
anything about...

788
00:45:14,344 --> 00:45:16,209
time travel?

789
00:45:20,584 --> 00:45:24,020
Dr. Monnitoff: Ah, a wormhole with
an Einstein-Rosen bridge,

790
00:45:24,088 --> 00:45:25,851
which is...

791
00:45:25,923 --> 00:45:27,948
theoretically,
a wormhole in space

792
00:45:28,025 --> 00:45:29,856
controlled by man.

793
00:45:32,162 --> 00:45:34,392
So, according to Hawking,

794
00:45:34,465 --> 00:45:37,696
a wormhole may be able
to provide a shortcut

795
00:45:37,768 --> 00:45:40,965
for jumping between two distant
regions of space-time.

796
00:45:41,038 --> 00:45:44,565
In order to travel back in time,
you have to have a big spaceship

797
00:45:44,675 --> 00:45:46,666
or something that can travel
faster than the speed of light?

798
00:45:46,777 --> 00:45:48,335
- Theoretically.
- And be able to find

799
00:45:48,412 --> 00:45:48,878
one of these wormholes?

800
00:45:48,946 --> 00:45:52,211
The basic principles
of time travel are there.

801
00:45:52,282 --> 00:45:53,510
You've got your vessel
and your portal,

802
00:45:53,584 --> 00:45:55,779
and your vessel could be
just about anything,

803
00:45:55,853 --> 00:45:58,413
- most likely a spacecraft.
- Like a DeLorean?

804
00:45:58,489 --> 00:46:00,184
Metal craft
of any kind.

805
00:46:00,257 --> 00:46:02,191
You know, I love that movie,
the way they shot it.

806
00:46:02,259 --> 00:46:03,658
It's so...

807
00:46:03,727 --> 00:46:05,524
Iike futuristic,
you know?

808
00:46:07,498 --> 00:46:08,965
Listen...

809
00:46:09,032 --> 00:46:11,296
Don't tell anybody
that I gave you this.

810
00:46:11,368 --> 00:46:13,928
The woman who wrote this
used to teach here.

811
00:46:14,004 --> 00:46:17,371
She was a nun many years
before that, but...

812
00:46:17,441 --> 00:46:20,205
then overnight,
she just--

813
00:46:20,310 --> 00:46:22,710
she became this
entirely different person.

814
00:46:22,780 --> 00:46:24,907
She up and left the church,
she wrote this book.

815
00:46:26,116 --> 00:46:28,880
She started
teaching science,

816
00:46:28,952 --> 00:46:30,544
right here in Middlesex.

817
00:46:30,621 --> 00:46:33,647
"The Philosophy
of Time Travel."

818
00:46:34,925 --> 00:46:36,415
Roberta Sparrow?

819
00:46:36,493 --> 00:46:38,586
That's right.

820
00:46:40,297 --> 00:46:42,322
(chuckles)
Come on.

821
00:46:42,399 --> 00:46:44,424
Roberta Sparrow?

822
00:47:04,221 --> 00:47:06,314
Roberta Sparrow.

823
00:47:07,691 --> 00:47:09,989
"Grandma Death."

824
00:47:11,128 --> 00:47:13,824
It's called "The Philosophy
of Time Travel."

825
00:47:15,032 --> 00:47:17,466
What does philosophy
have to do with time travel?

826
00:47:17,534 --> 00:47:19,695
- Let me see.
- Donnie: Guess who wrote it.

827
00:47:19,770 --> 00:47:21,294
Who?

828
00:47:21,371 --> 00:47:23,430
Roberta Sparrow?!

829
00:47:23,507 --> 00:47:25,634
Huh. She wrote a book.

830
00:47:25,742 --> 00:47:28,643
"Grandma Death"
wrote a book.

831
00:47:28,712 --> 00:47:30,680
That's a terrible
nickname.

832
00:47:30,781 --> 00:47:33,773
We almost hit her
with the car the other day.

833
00:47:35,052 --> 00:47:37,577
She lives up there
in that piece of crap house

834
00:47:37,654 --> 00:47:39,588
and you know
she's loaded. She's--

835
00:47:39,656 --> 00:47:41,351
Eddie:
Yeah, you're right.

836
00:47:41,425 --> 00:47:44,394
She used to be known
for her gem collection.

837
00:47:44,461 --> 00:47:46,725
Kids...

838
00:47:46,797 --> 00:47:49,163
used to go up there
all the time

839
00:47:49,233 --> 00:47:51,201
and try to steal stuff
from her.

840
00:47:51,268 --> 00:47:53,031
She became
a total recluse.

841
00:47:53,103 --> 00:47:56,470
Huh-- I didn't even know
she was alive

842
00:47:56,540 --> 00:47:59,168
till we damn near knocked
her down the other day.

843
00:48:02,012 --> 00:48:03,980
Donnie:
She was just standing there

844
00:48:04,081 --> 00:48:06,049
in the middle
of the road, frozen.

845
00:48:06,149 --> 00:48:08,049
So I got out of the car

846
00:48:08,118 --> 00:48:10,985
and I walked over to her
to see if she was okay.

847
00:48:11,054 --> 00:48:13,579
And she leaned over
and whispered in my ear.

848
00:48:13,657 --> 00:48:15,352
What did she say?

849
00:48:15,425 --> 00:48:17,518
I think Frank wants me
to go talk to her,

850
00:48:17,594 --> 00:48:19,391
because the last time
I saw him,

851
00:48:19,463 --> 00:48:21,590
he asked me if I knew
about time travel.

852
00:48:21,665 --> 00:48:23,565
She wrote a book
about it,

853
00:48:23,634 --> 00:48:25,261
so that can't be
a coincidence, right?

854
00:48:28,205 --> 00:48:30,696
Donnie, what did
Roberta Sparrow say to you?

855
00:48:32,643 --> 00:48:35,578
She said that every living
creature on Earth dies alone.

856
00:48:40,117 --> 00:48:42,483
How did that
make you feel?

857
00:48:46,356 --> 00:48:49,120
It reminded me
of my dog, Callie.

858
00:48:50,394 --> 00:48:52,362
She died
when I was eight,

859
00:48:52,429 --> 00:48:54,795
and she crawled
underneath...

860
00:48:54,865 --> 00:48:56,662
the porch.

861
00:48:59,603 --> 00:49:01,366
To die?

862
00:49:04,141 --> 00:49:05,870
To be alone.

863
00:49:07,811 --> 00:49:10,780
Do you feel
alone right now?

864
00:49:14,851 --> 00:49:16,716
I don't know.

865
00:49:19,156 --> 00:49:21,852
I'd like to believe
I'm not, but I just--

866
00:49:23,627 --> 00:49:26,095
I've just never
seen any proof, so I--

867
00:49:26,163 --> 00:49:28,529
I just don't
debate it anymore, you know?

868
00:49:28,598 --> 00:49:30,429
It's like I could
spend my whole life

869
00:49:30,500 --> 00:49:33,401
debating it over and over again,
weighing the pros and cons,

870
00:49:33,470 --> 00:49:35,995
and in the end, I still wouldn't
have any proof. So I just--

871
00:49:36,073 --> 00:49:37,973
I just don't debate it
any more.

872
00:49:38,041 --> 00:49:40,532
(laughs)
It's absurd.

873
00:49:44,348 --> 00:49:46,543
The search for God
is absurd?

874
00:49:49,086 --> 00:49:51,281
It is if everyone
dies alone.

875
00:49:52,589 --> 00:49:54,147
Does that scare you?

876
00:49:57,627 --> 00:49:59,925
I don't want
to be alone.

877
00:50:01,999 --> 00:50:04,331
(classical music plays)

878
00:50:13,176 --> 00:50:15,940
And so his tapes
have made me realize

879
00:50:16,046 --> 00:50:18,344
that for the last
39 years,

880
00:50:18,415 --> 00:50:21,077
I have been a prisoner
of my own fear.

881
00:50:21,151 --> 00:50:23,176
Fear?

882
00:50:23,253 --> 00:50:26,381
Rose, you have got
to meet this Jim Cunningham.

883
00:50:27,991 --> 00:50:30,255
I can't believe
he's single.

884
00:50:31,328 --> 00:50:33,319
Announcer #1 : And it has been
a disappointing night

885
00:50:33,397 --> 00:50:35,422
indeed for these
Super Bowl champions.

886
00:50:35,499 --> 00:50:37,364
Announcer #2:
You're right, Dan.

887
00:50:37,434 --> 00:50:39,197
Coach Joe Gibbs
is on the sidelines,

888
00:50:39,269 --> 00:50:41,760
water dripping off his glasses,
but he's gotta be thinking,

889
00:50:41,838 --> 00:50:44,466
"What happened?
What went wrong tonight?  "

890
00:50:44,541 --> 00:50:45,974
And here's the kick...

891
00:50:46,076 --> 00:50:47,737
(men groan)

892
00:50:47,844 --> 00:50:50,108
And it's no good.

893
00:50:50,180 --> 00:50:53,149
- Shit, we need a quarterback.
- And a miracle.

894
00:50:53,250 --> 00:50:55,514
Ronald:
We need to go for a safety.

895
00:50:56,987 --> 00:50:59,319
Announcer #1 : Mark Rypien
has some big shoes to fill,

896
00:50:59,389 --> 00:51:00,686
that's for certain.

897
00:51:00,757 --> 00:51:02,315
Announcer #2:
He sure does.

898
00:51:02,392 --> 00:51:05,293
So what the future holds
for this Super Bowl M.V.P.

899
00:51:05,362 --> 00:51:07,125
we're just gonna
have to wait and see.

900
00:51:09,866 --> 00:51:11,629
(sighs)

901
00:51:11,701 --> 00:51:13,396
- You guys want anything?
- Man: No.

902
00:51:13,470 --> 00:51:15,870
Announcer #1 : Darrell Green again
with a display of amazing speed...

903
00:51:15,939 --> 00:51:17,930
I'm gonna get a beer.

904
00:51:35,058 --> 00:51:36,685
...deep into the end zone.

905
00:51:36,760 --> 00:51:38,785
It's gonna be brought out
to the 20-yard line.

906
00:51:38,862 --> 00:51:41,729
First down and 10.

907
00:51:41,798 --> 00:51:44,824
Man: Good evening,
ladies and ghouls.

908
00:51:44,901 --> 00:51:47,529
Join us at the Middlesex
Pavilion Mall

909
00:51:47,604 --> 00:51:51,165
for the Middlesex
Halloween Haunt...

910
00:51:51,241 --> 00:51:53,232
(man laughs)

911
00:51:53,343 --> 00:51:55,140
(chuckles)

912
00:52:59,509 --> 00:53:02,000
(airplane engine whines)

913
00:53:15,926 --> 00:53:18,656
Gretchen: And what if
you could go back in time

914
00:53:18,728 --> 00:53:21,219
and take all those hours
of pain and darkness

915
00:53:21,298 --> 00:53:23,562
and replace them
with something better?

916
00:53:23,633 --> 00:53:25,533
Like images, or what?

917
00:53:25,602 --> 00:53:29,060
Yeah, like a Hawaiian sunset
or the Grand Canyon.

918
00:53:29,139 --> 00:53:31,664
Things that remind you
of how beautiful the world--

919
00:53:31,741 --> 00:53:33,709
We've been going together
for like, two weeks.

920
00:53:35,312 --> 00:53:36,711
Yeah?

921
00:53:38,949 --> 00:53:40,814
Well, I...

922
00:53:43,253 --> 00:53:45,221
Do you want to kiss me?

923
00:53:52,862 --> 00:53:55,353
- I-- I'm sorry.
- Look, Donnie, wait.

924
00:53:55,432 --> 00:53:58,924
- I like you a lot.
- I just want it to be...

925
00:53:59,002 --> 00:54:01,800
at a time when it...

926
00:54:01,871 --> 00:54:03,771
When what?

927
00:54:03,840 --> 00:54:06,104
When it
reminds me-- just...

928
00:54:07,744 --> 00:54:10,679
When it reminds you
how beautiful the world can be?

929
00:54:11,815 --> 00:54:13,612
Yeah.

930
00:54:14,684 --> 00:54:17,551
And right now there's some fat guy
over there staring at us.

931
00:54:24,094 --> 00:54:27,996
Thank you for seeing us
at such late notice.

932
00:54:28,064 --> 00:54:31,090
We both felt
that it was time

933
00:54:31,167 --> 00:54:32,725
for us to come in
and discuss...

934
00:54:32,802 --> 00:54:35,498
What I think is
going on with your son?

935
00:54:35,572 --> 00:54:38,063
Yes.

936
00:54:38,141 --> 00:54:41,304
Well, he's--

937
00:54:43,480 --> 00:54:45,038
You know
about his past,

938
00:54:45,148 --> 00:54:46,672
and he was suspended
from school

939
00:54:46,783 --> 00:54:49,251
for insulting
his gym teacher.

940
00:54:49,352 --> 00:54:51,286
I'm not really sure
that's a good example.

941
00:54:51,354 --> 00:54:53,982
I think he had just cause
to insult her.

942
00:54:54,057 --> 00:54:56,787
Rose, let me
just lay out

943
00:54:56,860 --> 00:54:58,259
what I believe
is happening here.

944
00:55:01,498 --> 00:55:03,796
Dr. Thurman:
Donnie's aggressive behavior...

945
00:55:05,402 --> 00:55:09,202
his increased detachment
from reality,

946
00:55:09,272 --> 00:55:13,265
seem to stem
from his inability to cope

947
00:55:13,376 --> 00:55:16,812
with the forces in the world
he perceives to be threatening.

948
00:55:22,686 --> 00:55:25,086
Has he ever told you
about his friend Frank?

949
00:55:29,793 --> 00:55:31,693
Frank?

950
00:55:31,761 --> 00:55:34,059
Yes, the giant
bunny rabbit.

951
00:55:35,332 --> 00:55:36,822
The what?

952
00:55:41,071 --> 00:55:43,539
I don't recall...

953
00:55:43,606 --> 00:55:46,541
him ever having
mentioned a rabbit.

954
00:55:46,643 --> 00:55:49,339
Donnie is experiencing
what is commonly called

955
00:55:49,446 --> 00:55:51,380
a daylight hallucination.

956
00:55:53,149 --> 00:55:55,583
(thumping)

957
00:55:55,652 --> 00:55:59,213
This is a common occurrence
among paranoid schizophrenics.

958
00:56:01,491 --> 00:56:03,322
What can we do?

959
00:56:03,393 --> 00:56:05,520
I would like to...

960
00:56:05,595 --> 00:56:07,324
do more hypnotherapy,

961
00:56:09,666 --> 00:56:12,032
and increase his medication.

962
00:56:14,003 --> 00:56:16,233
(grunts)

963
00:56:16,306 --> 00:56:18,240
(thunderclap)

964
00:56:18,308 --> 00:56:21,141
Rose:
Whatever will help him, really,

965
00:56:22,879 --> 00:56:24,608
because that's why
we're here.

966
00:56:24,681 --> 00:56:28,117
We just would like him
to experience some...

967
00:56:28,184 --> 00:56:30,152
relief.

968
00:56:30,253 --> 00:56:33,950
So if you think that
more medication will do that,

969
00:56:35,125 --> 00:56:39,391
then I think we should
give it a try.

970
00:56:39,462 --> 00:56:41,030
(tower bell rings)

971
00:56:41,030 --> 00:56:41,860
(tower bell rings)

972
00:56:51,107 --> 00:56:53,701
Donnie Darko.

973
00:56:56,546 --> 00:56:58,446
I know.

974
00:56:59,516 --> 00:57:02,212
(both laughing)

975
00:57:02,285 --> 00:57:05,083
(applause)

976
00:57:07,657 --> 00:57:09,215
Good morning,
you mongrels!

977
00:57:09,292 --> 00:57:10,589
Good morning.

978
00:57:10,660 --> 00:57:12,389
Is that all the gusto
you can muster?

979
00:57:12,462 --> 00:57:13,759
I said, "Good morning!"

980
00:57:13,830 --> 00:57:16,390
Good morning!

981
00:57:16,466 --> 00:57:18,297
Now that's a tiny,
tiny bit better.

982
00:57:18,368 --> 00:57:20,928
But I can still sense
some students out there

983
00:57:21,004 --> 00:57:24,167
who are actually afraid
to say, "Good morning!"

984
00:57:24,240 --> 00:57:27,539
- Good morning!
- Yeah, that's what I like to hear!

985
00:57:27,644 --> 00:57:30,772
Because entirely too many
young men and women today

986
00:57:30,880 --> 00:57:34,179
are completely paralyzed
by their fears.

987
00:57:35,418 --> 00:57:38,319
They surrender their bodies
to the temptation

988
00:57:38,388 --> 00:57:42,381
and destruction of drugs,
alcohol, and premarital sex.

989
00:57:43,860 --> 00:57:47,296
Now, I'm going to tell you
a little story today.

990
00:57:47,363 --> 00:57:49,627
It's a heartbreakingly
sad story

991
00:57:49,699 --> 00:57:51,724
about a young man

992
00:57:51,801 --> 00:57:54,031
whose life was
completely destroyed

993
00:57:54,103 --> 00:57:56,298
by these instruments
offear.

994
00:57:56,372 --> 00:57:58,237
A young man,

995
00:57:58,308 --> 00:58:01,573
searching for love
in all the wrong places.

996
00:58:02,679 --> 00:58:05,307
His name was Frank.

997
00:58:09,519 --> 00:58:11,316
Hi, my stepsister...

998
00:58:11,387 --> 00:58:14,185
Like, I sometimes worry
that she eats too much.

999
00:58:14,257 --> 00:58:16,691
- Shut up, Kim!
- Sweetheart, please.

1000
00:58:16,759 --> 00:58:18,784
How can I decide what I want
to be when I grow up?

1001
00:58:18,862 --> 00:58:20,159
That's a hard one.

1002
00:58:20,230 --> 00:58:21,424
(feedback squeals)

1003
00:58:21,498 --> 00:58:23,466
What do I do
to learn how to fight?

1004
00:58:23,533 --> 00:58:26,900
"What do I do
to learn how to fight?  "

1005
00:58:26,970 --> 00:58:30,906
Son, violence is
a product of fear.

1006
00:58:30,974 --> 00:58:32,737
Learn to truly love
yourself.

1007
00:58:32,842 --> 00:58:35,140
- Okay.
- Okay, get yourself up here.

1008
00:58:35,245 --> 00:58:38,009
- Okay.
- Jim: All right.

1009
00:58:41,284 --> 00:58:43,411
- Good morning.
- Good morning.

1010
00:58:43,486 --> 00:58:45,477
Um...

1011
00:58:45,555 --> 00:58:47,147
How much are they
paying you to be here?

1012
00:58:48,625 --> 00:58:50,115
Uh... excuse me?

1013
00:58:52,328 --> 00:58:53,989
Jim:
What is your name, son?

1014
00:58:54,063 --> 00:58:55,894
Gerald.

1015
00:58:55,965 --> 00:58:57,489
Well, Gerald,

1016
00:58:57,567 --> 00:58:59,091
I think you're afraid.

1017
00:58:59,168 --> 00:59:00,897
Are you telling us this stuff
so we can buy your book?

1018
00:59:00,970 --> 00:59:02,904
Because I got to tell you,
if you are,

1019
00:59:02,972 --> 00:59:04,872
that was some of the worst
advice I ever heard.

1020
00:59:04,941 --> 00:59:06,533
Jim: Do you see
how sad this is?

1021
00:59:06,643 --> 00:59:08,110
Do you want your sister
to lose weight?

1022
00:59:08,211 --> 00:59:09,940
Tell her to get off the couch,
stop eating Twinkies,

1023
00:59:10,046 --> 00:59:13,038
and maybe go out for field hockey.
You know what?

1024
00:59:13,149 --> 00:59:15,640
No one ever knows what they
want to be when they grow up.

1025
00:59:15,752 --> 00:59:18,448
It takes a little while
to find that out.

1026
00:59:18,521 --> 00:59:20,421
Right, Jim?
And you...

1027
00:59:20,490 --> 00:59:22,583
Yeah, you.

1028
00:59:23,693 --> 00:59:26,719
Sick of some jerk shoving
your head down the toilet?

1029
00:59:26,796 --> 00:59:28,957
Maybe you should
lift some weights

1030
00:59:29,032 --> 00:59:31,432
or take a karate lesson.
And the next time he tries to do it,

1031
00:59:31,501 --> 00:59:33,366
you kick him
in the balls.

1032
00:59:33,436 --> 00:59:35,631
(adults gasp;
students laugh)

1033
00:59:35,705 --> 00:59:37,696
(Jim chuckles)
Son. Do you see this?

1034
